x ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">With the rising popularity of pa=
tterns such as microservices, it is becoming more and more common that the =
API that your client application is calling, isn=E2=80=99t the API that is =
going to be performing the requested functionality. Instead, you could be c=
alling an API gateway.</p><p id=3D"0b1a" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb=
 lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly =
eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">OAuth is all about delegation. It all=
ows a client application to ask resource owner (a user) for permission to a=
ccess a protected resource (an HTTP API) on their behalf. It is a delegatio=
n protocol.</p><p id=3D"a0f0" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b=
 le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data=
-selectable-paragraph=3D"">So, what happens when a client application commu=
nicates with a protected resource that itself then needs to interact with o=
ther protected resources? How do we keep this request acting on the user=E2=
=80=99s behalf? How do we do this securely without getting the user involve=
d again?</p><p id=3D"484f" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le=
 lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-se=
lectable-paragraph=3D"">Let=E2=80=99s start by looking at a typical scenari=
o where I=E2=80=99ve seen this issue, and then look at some solutions prese=
nted by the community and the OAuth Working Group.</p><h1 id=3D"80fe" class=
=3D"lz ma ev be mb mc md me mf mg mh mi mj mk ml mm mn mo mp mq mr ms mt mu=
 mv mw bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Scenario: API Gateway</h1><p id=
