selectable-paragraph=3D"">Let=E2=80=99s play the authorization request thro=
ugh in our heads. We=E2=80=99re asking the user to access a particular API =
on their behalf, API1 (the API gateway). So API1 is the intended audience o=
f the access token. However, we=E2=80=99re now using that token to call API=
2. This means API2 must accept tokens intended for API1, meaning that they =
share an audience and scopes, breaking our authorization model.</p><p id=3D=
"7d58" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk l=
l lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=
=3D"">If we approach it from the other angle and say that API1 must accept =
tokens from API2, we=E2=80=99re again breaking our authorization model, but=
 now our gateway will have to support many different audiences and scopes.<=
/p><p id=3D"9f2e" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh=
 li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-=
paragraph=3D"">We could maybe mitigate this by saying the token has multipl=
e audiences. However, is API1 even allowed to read the token? Could it be t=
hat this token is a structured piece of data that contains sensitive data t=
hat API1 is not allowed to read?</p><p id=3D"1a5b" class=3D"pw-post-body-pa=
ragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv=
 lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Even then, no matter which =
way you look at it, we=E2=80=99ve now opened API2 from being accessed direc=
tly. If it is or ever becomes publicly accessible, an access token that sho=
uld be used via the gateway can now be used to directly access API2. We hav=
e the potential to bypass API1.</p><p id=3D"82ae" class=3D"pw-post-body-par=
agraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv =
lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">When using this approach, we=
 are not really using delegation but rather impersonation.</p><h1 id=3D"c0b=
5" class=3D"lz ma ev be mb mc md me mf mg mh mi mj mk ml mm mn mo mp mq mr =
ms mt mu mv mw bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Machine to Machine Commun=
ication with the Client Credentials Grant Type</h1><p id=3D"522a" class=3D"=
pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le mx lg lh li my lk ll lm mz lo lp lq=
 na ls lt lu nb lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">What we coul=
d do is have API1 call our authorization server and get a new token using t=
he client credentials grant type. This would mean the initial token authori=
zed by the user would now be scoped to API1, but now we lose track of the d=
elegating user.</p><figure class=3D"nf ng nh ni nj nk nc nd paragraph-image=
