bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">To make this approach work, we would hav=
e to allow API1 to access any user=E2=80=99s data on API2. We would also ha=
ve to move API2=E2=80=99s user-based authorization rules to within API1. If=
 you want to ensure that the user can only access their own data within API=
2, the only place with enough information to make that decision is now API1=
.</p><p id=3D"f11a" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg =
lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectabl=
e-paragraph=3D"">This <em class=3D"nu">might</em> work for a high trust sce=
nario, but as soon as you want API2 to be accessed by other applications, y=
ou would have to replicate these authorization rules within each consuming =
application.</p><h1 id=3D"1ea0" class=3D"lz ma ev be mb mc md me mf mg mh m=
i mj mk ml mm mn mo mp mq mr ms mt mu mv mw bj" data-selectable-paragraph=
