ragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv=
 lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">With this style, we are wal=
king the line between delegation and impersonation. While we can get a toke=
n that includes the user and API1, API2 has no way of knowing if the differ=
ence between a token issued via user interaction and a token issued as a re=
sult of delegation.</p><h1 id=3D"c8e2" class=3D"lz ma ev be mb mc md me mf =
mg mh mi mj mk ml mm mn mo mp mq mr ms mt mu mv mw bj" data-selectable-para=
graph=3D"">JWT Bearer Authorization Grant (RFC 7523)</h1><p id=3D"4cad" cla=
ss=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le mx lg lh li my lk ll lm mz lo=
 lp lq na ls lt lu nb lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">From t=
he specification, the JWT Bearer Authorization Grant is:</p><blockquote cla=
ss=3D"og oh oi"><p id=3D"492b" class=3D"lb lc nu ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk =
ll oj ln lo lp ok lr ls lt ol lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=
=3D""><em class=3D"ev">[A way for] a JWT Bearer Token can be used to reques=
t an access token when a client wishes to utilize an existing trust relatio=
nship, [=E2=80=A6] without a direct user-approval step at the authorization=
 server =E2=80=94</em> <a class=3D"af om" href=3D"https://tools.ietf.org/ht=
ml/rfc7523#section-1" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank">https=
://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7523#section-1</a></p></blockquote><p id=3D"1174"=
 class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm l=
n lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">A =
similar approach is defined for using SAML assertions to get access tokens =
in <a class=3D"af om" href=3D"https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7522" rel=3D"n=
oopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank">RFC 7522</a>.</p><p id=3D"2f13" cla=
ss=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo=
 lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">While =
at first seeming similar to the custom delegation approach we just discusse=
d, the authorization grant style is not suitable for delegation in our scen=
ario.</p><p id=3D"ae88" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf=
 lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selec=
table-paragraph=3D"">This is mainly because the intended audience of the JW=
T bearer or SAML assertion <a class=3D"af om" href=3D"https://tools.ietf.or=
g/html/rfc7523#section-3" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank">m=
ust be the authorization server</a>. From what I=E2=80=99ve seen in past im=
plementations, this authorization grant style is most suited towards swappi=
ng tokens and assertions issued by a different authorization server/identit=
y provider, for tokens issued by our authorization server.</p><p id=3D"1e52=
" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm =
ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">M=
y other gripe with these authorization grants is that they do not require e=
ither client authentication or even a client ID. By not requiring a client =
identification we=E2=80=99re removing much of the delegation features we in=
troduced with our custom grant type. If no identification was provided, who=
 are we issuing the new token to? How does this affect or authorization pol=
icies within API2?</p><p id=3D"43ea" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc =
ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo b=
j" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Much like our custom delegation grant typ=
e, this style also errs more towards impersonation that delegation.</p><p i=
d=3D"8a19" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj =
lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragra=
ph=3D"">And obviously, if you are using SAML 2.0 or JWT access tokens, this=
 authorization grant type is unavailable to you. At least not in the form o=
f a formalized specification.</p><h1 id=3D"5faa" class=3D"lz ma ev be mb mc=
 md me mf mg mh mi mj mk ml mm mn mo mp mq mr ms mt mu mv mw bj" data-selec=
table-paragraph=3D"">OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange</h1><p id=3D"0569" class=3D"p=
w-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le mx lg lh li my lk ll lm mz lo lp lq =
na ls lt lu nb lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The OAuth Wor=
king Group are <a class=3D"af om" href=3D"https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft=
-ietf-oauth-token-exchange-15" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_bla=
nk">working on a specification</a> to formalize the above delegation scenar=
io, currently called OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange. This draft seems to have bee=
n floating around for a while, but based on recent activity (2018), it seem=
s to have picked up steam again.</p><p id=3D"b325" class=3D"pw-post-body-pa=
ragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv=
 lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">This specification looks to=
 achieve the same as our custom grant; however, it also takes into account =
a few other delegation scenarios.</p><p id=3D"ba82" class=3D"pw-post-body-p=
aragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu l=
v lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">So, a typical request woul=
d look like:</p><pre class=3D"nf ng nh ni nj nw nx ny nz ax oa bj"><span id=
=3D"32c8" class=3D"ob ma ev nx b gw oc od l hn oe" data-selectable-paragrap=
h=3D""><strong class=3D"nx ew">POST</strong> /token<br>Host: auth.example.c=
om<br>Authorization: Basic YXBpMTpzb21lc2VjcmV0<br>Content-Type: applicatio=
n/x-www-form-urlencoded</span><span id=3D"316d" class=3D"ob ma ev nx b gw o=
f od l hn oe" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">grant_type=3Durn:ietf:params:o=
auth:grant-type:token-exchange<br>&amp;resource=3Dhttps://backend.example.c=
om/api2<br>&amp;subject_token=3DaccVkjcJyb4BWCxGsndESCJQbdFMogUC5PbRDqceLTC=
<br>&amp;subject_token_type=3Durn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:access_token=
</span></pre><p id=3D"6388" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b l=
e lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-s=
electable-paragraph=3D"">Where the subject token is the original token, del=
egated to API1, and token type is the type of that token (in this case an a=
ccess token).</p><p id=3D"b000" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld=
 b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" da=
ta-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The token response is then slightly different =
than what we are used to:</p><pre class=3D"nf ng nh ni nj nw nx ny nz ax oa=
 bj"><span id=3D"4c95" class=3D"ob ma ev nx b gw oc od l hn oe" data-select=
able-paragraph=3D"">HTTP/1.1 200 OK<br>Content-Type: application/json<br>Ca=
che-Control: no-cache, no-store</span><span id=3D"1a4d" class=3D"ob ma ev n=
x b gw of od l hn oe" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">{<br>  "access_token":=
 "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJzdWIiOiIxMjMiLCJhdWQiOiJhcGkyIiwi=
aWF0IjoxNTE2MjM5MDIyfQ.xdnkfqb5HmXGxI_eE6AQhbykHsLS9PVXYPWhGP8q80I",<br>  "=
issued_token_type":"urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:access_token",<br>  "t=
oken_type":"Bearer", "expires_in":60<br>}</span></pre><p id=3D"8f7b" class=
=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo l=
p lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The <cod=
e class=3D"cw on oo op nx b">issued_token_type</code> tells API1 (now actin=
g as a client application) that it has sent back an access token, informing=
 it how to use the contents of the response. In this case, it=E2=80=99s sim=
ply to use to access a protected resource (API2) and not to read or validat=
e.</p><p id=3D"5151" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg=
 lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectab=
le-paragraph=3D"">The defined values for <code class=3D"cw on oo op nx b">i=
ssued_token_type</code> are:</p><ul class=3D""><li id=3D"e516" class=3D"lb =
lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll oj ln lo lp ok lr ls lt ol lv lw lx ly o=
q or os bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D""><code class=3D"cw on oo op nx b"=
>urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:access_token</code></li><li id=3D"8de5" c=
lass=3D"lb lc ev ld b le ot lg lh li ou lk ll oj ov lo lp ok ow ls lt ol ox=
 lw lx ly oq or os bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D""><code class=3D"cw on =
oo op nx b">urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:refresh_token</code></li><li i=
d=3D"fbce" class=3D"lb lc ev ld b le ot lg lh li ou lk ll oj ov lo lp ok ow=
 ls lt ol ox lw lx ly oq or os bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D""><code cla=
ss=3D"cw on oo op nx b">urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:id_token</code></l=
i><li id=3D"ccdc" class=3D"lb lc ev ld b le ot lg lh li ou lk ll oj ov lo l=
p ok ow ls lt ol ox lw lx ly oq or os bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D""><c=
ode class=3D"cw on oo op nx b">urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:saml1</code=
></li><li id=3D"8050" class=3D"lb lc ev ld b le ot lg lh li ou lk ll oj ov =
lo lp ok ow ls lt ol ox lw lx ly oq or os bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"=
"><code class=3D"cw on oo op nx b">urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:saml2</=
code></li><li id=3D"8626" class=3D"lb lc ev ld b le ot lg lh li ou lk ll oj=
 ov lo lp ok ow ls lt ol ox lw lx ly oq or os bj" data-selectable-paragraph=
=3D""><code class=3D"cw on oo op nx b">urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt=
</code> (just a JWT, not an access token)</li></ul><p id=3D"f0cf" class=3D"=
pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq=
 lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">This delegat=
ion flow also comes with some defined JWT claim types, most notable of whic=
h are <code class=3D"cw on oo op nx b">act</code> and <code class=3D"cw on =
oo op nx b">may_act</code>.</p><p id=3D"4b18" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragra=
ph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw l=
x ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The Actor claim type (<code clas=
s=3D"cw on oo op nx b">act</code>) allows us to express that delegation has=
 taken place by using a JWT claim set about the current actor.</p><pre clas=
s=3D"nf ng nh ni nj nw nx ny nz ax oa bj"><span id=3D"bb1d" class=3D"ob ma =
ev nx b gw oc od l hn oe" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">{<br>  "aud":"http=
s://consumer.example.com",<br>  "iss":"https://issuer.example.com",<br>  "e=
xp":1443904177,<br>  "nbf":1443904077,<br>  "sub":"user",<br>  "act": { <br=
>    "sub":"admin"<br>  } <br>}</span></pre><p id=3D"33e8" class=3D"pw-post=
-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls =
lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Here the initial to=
ken was issued to <code class=3D"cw on oo op nx b">user</code>, and we=E2=
=80=99re still acting on their behalf. But, by using the <code class=3D"cw =
on oo op nx b">act</code> claim set, we can show that <code class=3D"cw on =
oo op nx b">admin</code> is the current actor. At the very least this would=
 be valuable for audit trails.</p><p id=3D"61f3" class=3D"pw-post-body-para=
graph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv l=
w lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Actor claim sets can be chain=
ed (an act claim set within an act claim set) to present a clear chain of d=
elegation; however, it=E2=80=99s always the top-level user and their direct=
 actor that should be used for authorization policies.</p><p id=3D"b833" cl=
ass=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln l=
o lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The M=
ay Act For claim type (<code class=3D"cw on oo op nx b">may_act</code>) all=
ows us to explicitly state who is allowed to act on someone=E2=80=99s behal=
f. For instance, if we wanted to explicitly state that <code class=3D"cw on=
 oo op nx b">admin</code> is authorized to act on <code class=3D"cw on oo o=
p nx b">user</code>=E2=80=99s behalf:</p><pre class=3D"nf ng nh ni nj nw nx=
 ny nz ax oa bj"><span id=3D"d52e" class=3D"ob ma ev nx b gw oc od l hn oe"=
 data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">{<br>  "aud":"https://consumer.example.com"=
,<br>  "iss":"https://issuer.example.com",<br>  "exp":1443904177,<br>  "nbf=
":1443904077,<br>  "sub":"user",<br>  "may_act": { <br>    "sub":"admin"<br=
>  }<br>}</span></pre><p id=3D"ce1f" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph lb lc =
ev ld b le lf lg lh li lj lk ll lm ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly eo b=
j" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">This means in our scenario, the process w=
ould now look like the following, where we are getting a new token that inc=
